.TH SDL_ALIGNED 3 "SDL 3.5.0" "Simple Directmedia Layer" "SDL3 FUNCTIONS"
.SH NAME
SDL_ALIGNED \- A macro to specify data alignment.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <SDL3/SDL_begin_code.h>
.PP
.BI "#define SDL_ALIGNED(x) __attribute__((aligned(x)))
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
This informs the compiler that a given datatype or variable must be aligned to a specific byte count\[char46]
.PP
For example:

.IP
.EX
// make sure this is struct is aligned to 16 bytes for SIMD access.
typedef struct {
   float x, y, z, w;
} SDL_ALIGNED(16) MySIMDAlignedData;

// make sure this one field in a struct is aligned to 16 bytes for SIMD access.
typedef struct {
   SomeStuff stuff;
   float SDL_ALIGNED(16) position[4];
   SomeOtherStuff other_stuff;
} MyStruct;

// make sure this variable is aligned to 32 bytes.
int SDL_ALIGNED(32) myval = 0;
.EE
.PP

Alignment is only guaranteed for things the compiler places: local variables on the stack and global/static variables\[char46] To dynamically allocate something that respects this alignment, use
.BR SDL_aligned_alloc ()
or some other mechanism\[char46]
.PP
On compilers without alignment support, this macro is defined to an invalid symbol, to make it clear that the current compiler is likely to generate incorrect code when it sees this macro\[char46]

.SH MACRO PARAMETERS
.TP
.I x
the byte count to align to, so the data\(aqs address will be a multiple of this value\[char46]
.SH AVAILABILITY
This macro is available since SDL 3\[char46]4\[char46]0\[char46]

